<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomé Pires</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1465–c. 1540</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomé Pires was a Portuguese apothecary and writer who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> from 1512 to 1515. His Suma Oriental, written there, is the most detailed European account of the trade and ports of maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> in the early 16th century, describing lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In 1517 he led the first Portuguese embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which failed; he was detained and died there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tome-pires.jpg" alt="Tomé Pires" width="233" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tom%C3%A9_Pires_stamp.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
